A shelter keeps a generic reference to a specific animal:
class Animal { String speak() { return "generic"; } }
class Dog extends Animal { String speak() { return "woof"; } }
Animal a = new Dog();
System.out.println(a.speak());
What is printed?

A middle class builds its tag by calling up to its parent:
class Base { String tag() { return "B"; } }
class Mid extends Base { String tag() { return "M" + super.tag(); } }
Mid m = new Mid();
System.out.println(m.tag());
What is printed?

A subclass declares a same-named static method on a base class that also has one:
class Base { static String name() { return "base"; } }
class Sub extends Base { static String name() { return "sub"; } }
Base b = new Sub();
System.out.println(b.name());
What is printed?

A base class keeps its identifier private and a subclass declares a public method of the same name:
class Base {
  private String id() { return "base"; }
  public String show() { return id(); }
}
class Sub extends Base { public String id() { return "sub"; } }
System.out.println(new Sub().show());
What is printed?

A subclass adds a differently typed method of the same name and a call is made through a base-typed reference:
class Animal { String who(String s) { return "A"; } }
class Dog extends Animal { String who(int n) { return "D"; } }
Animal a = new Dog();
System.out.println(a.who(7));
What happens when this is compiled and run?

A subclass declares a field with the same name as one in its superclass, and the superclass reports the field:
class Animal { String name = "generic"; void report() { System.out.println(name); } }
class Dog extends Animal { String name = "rex"; }
Animal a = new Dog();
a.report();
What is printed?

Two different animals share one array and one call site:
class Animal { String sound() { return "..."; } }
class Dog extends Animal { String sound() { return "woof"; } }
class Cat extends Animal { String sound() { return "meow"; } }
Animal[] pets = { new Dog(), new Cat() };
for (Animal p : pets) System.out.print(p.sound() + " ");
What is printed?

A greeting interface supplies a default and one implementer replaces it:
interface Greeter { default String hi() { return "hi"; } }
class Formal implements Greeter { public String hi() { return "hello"; } }
Greeter g = new Formal();
System.out.println(g.hi());
What is printed?

A class implements two unrelated interfaces that both declare the same default method:
interface A { default String tag() { return "A"; } }
interface B { default String tag() { return "B"; } }
class C implements A, B { }
System.out.println(new C().tag());
What happens?

A subclass constructor tries to hand one of its own fields to the superclass constructor:
class Base { Base(int n) { System.out.print(n); } }
class Sub extends Base {
    int size = 5;
    Sub() { super(size); }
}
What happens when this is compiled?

Three classes form a chain and then a method runs:
class A { A() { System.out.print("A"); } }
class B extends A { B() { System.out.print("B"); } void go() { System.out.print("go"); } }
class C extends B { C() { System.out.print("C"); } }
new C().go();
What is printed?

A superclass constructor reads a field while a subclass initializes one:
class Base { int x; Base() { System.out.println(x); } }
class Derived extends Base {
    int y = 5;
    Derived() { System.out.println(y); }
}
new Derived();
What does the program print?

A superclass has only a one-argument constructor and the subclass declares none of its own arguments:
class Base { Base(int n) { System.out.print(n); } }
class Sub extends Base { Sub() { System.out.print(9); } }
new Sub();
What happens?

A superclass constructor calls a method the subclass overrides and reports what it returns:
class Base {
  String label = "B";
  String tag() { return "base:" + label; }
  Base() { System.out.println(tag()); }
}
class Sub extends Base {
  String label = "S";
  String tag() { return "sub:" + label; }
}
new Sub();
What is printed?

A base class returns the general type and the subclass wants to return its own type instead:
class Animal { Animal spawn() { return new Animal(); } }
class Dog extends Animal {
    ______ spawn() { return new Dog(); }
}
Which completion makes this a legal covariant override?

An override narrows the return type and a caller holds a base-typed reference:
class Animal { String tag() { return "A"; } Animal spawn() { return new Animal(); } }
class Dog extends Animal { String tag() { return "D"; } Dog spawn() { return new Dog(); } }
Animal a = new Dog();
Animal x = a.spawn();
Dog y = a.spawn();
System.out.println(x.tag() + y.tag());
What happens?

A class narrows an interface return type and a caller asks the returned object to describe itself:
interface Source { Object fetch(); }
class Impl implements Source { public String fetch() { return "s"; } }
Source s = new Impl();
System.out.println(s.fetch().getClass().getSimpleName());
What is printed?

A row class defines equality with a less-than comparison on its position:
class Row {
  final int index;
  Row(int index) { this.index = index; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    return o instanceof Row && this.index < ((Row) o).index;
  }
}
System.out.println(new Row(1).equals(new Row(2)) + "," + new Row(2).equals(new Row(1)));
What is printed?

A subclass redefines equals on a new field and never mentions the superclass:
class Item {
  int id;
  Item(int id) { this.id = id; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Item && ((Item) o).id == id; }
}
class Gift extends Item {
  String wrap;
  Gift(int id, String wrap) { super(id); this.wrap = wrap; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Gift && ((Gift) o).wrap.equals(wrap); }
}
Item a = new Item(1);
Item b = new Gift(1, "red");
System.out.println(a.equals(b) + "," + b.equals(a));
What is printed?
